    <h2>Letter from the Editor-in-Chief</h2>
    <p>
      When our founders launched <em>The Argosy</em>, their aim was simple but ambitious: to give students a real
      newsroom — a place where ideas, stories, and debate could flourish. They wanted more than a school paper.
      They wanted a forum where curiosity meets craft, where sharp reporting and thoughtful essays live side by side.
      Three years, several hundred pieces, and one graduation season later, I can report that they built it —
      and then handed it over, which is the part institutions usually get wrong, and they got right.
    </p>
    <p>
      An argosy is a merchant ship: a vessel built to carry valuable cargo long distances, through weather.
      Ships are defined by what they carry, not by who happens to be at the helm. What this one carries hasn't
      changed with the crew: we take the world seriously before anyone asks us to, we show our work — wrong
      predictions and all — and we practice disagreement in public, by name, with people we intend to keep liking.
      Our founding cohort now reads us from campuses across two continents, and their standard remains the
      standard.
    </p>
    <p>
      To every writer, editor, and mentor who gave these pages their heartbeat — and to the readers who argue
      with us in the comments and come back anyway — thank you. The conversation is the paper. Welcome aboard.
    </p>
    <p class="sig">— <strong>Ethan Yang</strong>, Editor-in-Chief</p>
  </div>
</section>
